// The tables the two newer block formats are read with.
//
// A block of BC7 or BC6H names a shape: a four-by-four square cut into one, two
// or three regions, and a pixel takes its endpoints from the region it falls
// in. The shapes are numbered, and the numbers are these tables — sixty-four
// for two regions and sixty-four for three in BC7, thirty-two for two regions
// in BC6H. Nothing computes them; they are drawn by hand in the specification
// and copied here.
//
// The high bit marks the fix-up index, which is stored one bit narrower than
// the rest: the first pixel of a subset always has index nought, so the bit that
// would always be nought is not written. A byte is used rather than two smaller
// tables because the values are read in a loop that runs on every block.

///|
/// The shapes of BC7, two regions first and then three, sixteen entries each.
///
